### What is Cache-Aside?

Cache-Aside is the most common pattern and the default for most services. The application controls everything: on a read, check the cache; on a miss, load from the database, write to the cache with a TTL, and return.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CacheAsideExample {

    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new HashMap<>();
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> database = Map.of(
            "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("75000.00"),
            "SAV3002", new BigDecimal("120000.00"));

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        BigDecimal cached = cache.get(accountId);
        if (cached != null) {
            System.out.println("Cache HIT for " + accountId);
            return cached;
        }
        System.out.println("Cache MISS for " + accountId + ", loading from DB");
        BigDecimal fromDb = database.get(accountId);
        if (fromDb != null) {
            cache.put(accountId, fromDb); // populate cache
        }
        return fromDb;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CacheAsideExample svc = new CacheAsideExample();
        svc.getBalance("SAV2001"); // miss
        svc.getBalance("SAV2001"); // hit
        svc.getBalance("SAV3002"); // miss
    }
}
```

```text
Cache MISS for SAV2001, loading from DB
Cache HIT for SAV2001
Cache MISS for SAV3002, loading from DB
```

### What is Read-Through?

Read-Through moves the load-on-miss responsibility into the cache itself. The application only asks the cache, and the cache invokes a configured loader on a miss.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.LoadingCache;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

public class ReadThroughExample {

    // Caffeine LoadingCache is a Read-Through cache
    private final LoadingCache<String, BigDecimal> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(10_000)
            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
            .build(accountId -> loadFromDatabase(accountId)); // loader

    private BigDecimal loadFromDatabase(String accountId) {
        System.out.println("Loader: fetching " + accountId + " from DB");
        return new BigDecimal("75000.00"); // simulated
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return cache.get(accountId); // miss triggers loader transparently
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReadThroughExample svc = new ReadThroughExample();
        System.out.println("Balance: " + svc.getBalance("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Balance: " + svc.getBalance("SAV2001")); // hit, no loader call
    }
}
```

```text
Loader: fetching SAV2001 from DB
Balance: 75000.00
Balance: 75000.00
```

### What are Write-Through and Write-Behind?

Write-Through writes to both the cache and the database synchronously. Write-Behind writes to the cache and flushes to the database asynchronously.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class WritePatternsExample {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    // Write-Through: sync write to both
    public void writeThrough(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        cache.put(accountId, balance);             // cache update
        System.out.println("DB write (sync): " + accountId + " = " + balance);
        // Both updated before returning. Consistent but slower.
    }

    // Write-Behind: cache now, DB later
    public void writeBehind(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        cache.put(accountId, balance);             // cache update (instant)
        System.out.println("Queued for DB flush: " + accountId + " = " + balance);
        // Background thread flushes to DB later. Fast but risks data loss.
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WritePatternsExample svc = new WritePatternsExample();
        svc.writeThrough("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("80000.00"));
        svc.writeBehind("SAV3002", new BigDecimal("95000.00"));
    }
}
```

```text
DB write (sync): SAV2001 = 80000.00
Queued for DB flush: SAV3002 = 95000.00
```

### How do you compare the caching patterns?

| Pattern | Who Loads? | Write Path | Consistency | Durability Risk | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cache-Aside | App code | App invalidates cache | Eventual (TTL) | None (DB is truth) | Read-heavy, most services |
| Read-Through | Cache (loader) | Separate | Eventual (TTL) | None | Centralized loading logic |
| Write-Through | Cache | Sync to both | Strong | None | Reads must see latest write |
| Write-Behind | Cache | Async to DB | Eventual | Yes (cache failure) | High-write, counters, metrics |

### When do you evolve from Caffeine to Redis?

Caffeine is in-process: a lookup is a method call with no network hop, nanosecond speed. But each instance has its own private cache. When you run multiple instances, they drift: a write on one instance does not invalidate the stale copy on another.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class CaffeineVsRedis {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Single Instance ===");
        System.out.println("Caffeine: nanosecond lookups, perfect for one instance");

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== Multiple Instances ===");
        System.out.println("Instance-1 Caffeine: SAV2001 = 75000 (stale!)");
        System.out.println("Instance-2 Caffeine: SAV2001 = 80000 (updated)");
        System.out.println("Problem: instance-1 never heard about the update");

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== Fix: Shared Redis ===");
        System.out.println("Redis: SAV2001 = 80000 (shared, all instances see it)");
        System.out.println("Cost: network hop per access (~0.5ms vs ~50ns)");
    }
}
```

```text
=== Single Instance ===
Caffeine: nanosecond lookups, perfect for one instance

=== Multiple Instances ===
Instance-1 Caffeine: SAV2001 = 75000 (stale!)
Instance-2 Caffeine: SAV2001 = 80000 (updated)
Problem: instance-1 never heard about the update

=== Fix: Shared Redis ===
Redis: SAV2001 = 80000 (shared, all instances see it)
Cost: network hop per access (~0.5ms vs ~50ns)
```

### What is multi-level caching (L1 Caffeine + L2 Redis)?

The best of both worlds: a small, short-lived Caffeine L1 in each instance sits in front of a shared Redis L2. The hottest keys are served at nanosecond speed from L1 while L2 keeps instances roughly consistent.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MultiLevelCache {

    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> l1 = new HashMap<>();  // Caffeine (per-instance)
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> l2 = new HashMap<>();  // Redis (shared)
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> db = Map.of("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("75000.00"));

    public BigDecimal get(String accountId) {
        BigDecimal val = l1.get(accountId);
        if (val != null) { System.out.println("L1 HIT"); return val; }

        val = l2.get(accountId);
        if (val != null) { System.out.println("L2 HIT"); l1.put(accountId, val); return val; }

        System.out.println("DB LOAD");
        val = db.get(accountId);
        l2.put(accountId, val);
        l1.put(accountId, val);
        return val;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MultiLevelCache cache = new MultiLevelCache();
        cache.get("SAV2001"); // DB LOAD
        cache.get("SAV2001"); // L1 HIT
    }
}
```

```text
DB LOAD
L1 HIT
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most dangerous mistake is caching the authoritative balance used to authorize a debit. A cache with a TTL is eventually consistent by definition. You cache a displayed balance but you always read the authoritative balance from the database when authorizing a debit.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CachingMistakes {

    // WRONG: using cached balance for debit authorization
    static void wrongDebit(BigDecimal cachedBalance, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (cachedBalance.compareTo(amount) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("WRONG: approved debit based on stale cached balance");
            // Cache might be 2 seconds old, actual balance could be lower
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: read authoritative balance from DB for debit
    static void correctDebit(BigDecimal dbBalance, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (dbBalance.compareTo(amount) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("CORRECT: approved debit based on DB (source of truth)");
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you handle cache invalidation on writes in Cache-Aside?
- When is Write-Behind acceptable for financial data?
- How do you prevent a cache stampede when a hot key expires?
- What is the consistency trade-off of the L1 TTL in multi-level caching?
- How does Redis publish/subscribe help with cross-instance invalidation?
